[Event "Tal – Smyslov, Candidates 1959 (against the Caro-Kann)"]
[White "Tal"]
[Black "Smyslov"]
[Date "1959.??.??"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{For you as a Caro-Kann player: don't open the centre while behind in development.
Smyslov, a former world champion, played the Caro-Kann. Tal's quiet 2.d3 led to
opposite-side castling and a storm.} 1. e4 c6 2. d3 {A calm King's Indian set-up. It
sidesteps Caro-Kann theory.} 2... d5 3. Nd2 e5 4. Ngf3 Nd7 5. d4 dxe4 6. Nxe4 exd4 7. Qxd4
Ngf6 8. Bg5 Be7 9. O-O-O {Opposite-side castling. Both kings are targets, and the faster
attack wins.} 9... O-O 10. Nd6 {The knight lands on d6, hitting b7 and c8. Black's
queenside is tied up.} 10... Qa5 11. Bc4 b5 12. Bd2 Qa6 13. Nf5 Bd8 14. Qh4 {Question:
Your bishop on c4 is attacked. Save it, or attack? Tal ignores the bishop and brings the
queen to the kingside. The engine says the position is balanced. Black had to find one
exact defence, and there were many ways to go wrong.} 14... bxc4 15. Qg5 {Threatens Qxg7
mate. Black has to block with ...Nh5.} 15... Nh5 16. Nh6+ Kh8 17. Qxh5 Qxa2 18. Bc3 Nf6
19. Qxf7 {Question: Look for a sequence of checks that ends in a fork. The queen sacrifice
wins. After 19...Qa1+ 20.Kd2 Rxf7 21.Nxf7+ Kg8 22.Rxa1 Kxf7 23.Ne5+, Tal comes out with a
decisive material advantage (engine about +4).} 19... Qa1+ 20. Kd2 Rxf7 21. Nxf7+ Kg8 22.
Rxa1 Kxf7 23. Ne5+ Ke6 24. Nxc6 Ne4+ 25. Ke3 Bb6+ 26. Bd4 {Black resigned. Lesson for your
Caro-Kann: Smyslov opened the centre with ...e5 and ...exd4 before his pieces were out,
and Tal's knights jumped into d6 and f5.} 1-0

[Event "Tal – Bronstein, USSR Championship 1971 (Panov Attack)"]
[White "Tal"]
[Black "Bronstein"]
[Date "1971.??.??"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{In the Panov, Black must castle before grabbing anything. An exchange sacrifice for
lasting pressure. The Panov Attack against your Caro-Kann. Bronstein kept his king in the
centre one move too long.} 1. e4 c6 2. c4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Bg5
{Pressure on d5 and f6, the main danger for Black in the Panov.} 6... Bg4 7. Be2 Bxe2 8.
Ngxe2 dxc4 9. d5 {Tal gives a pawn for time. The black king has not castled.} 9... Ne5 10.
O-O h6 11. Bf4 Ng6 12. Qa4+ {Wins the pawn back with tempo.} 12... Qd7 13. Qxc4 Rc8 14.
Qb3 e5 15. dxe6 Qxe6 16. Qxb7 Bc5 17. Nd4 Bxd4 18. Rae1 O-O 19. Rxe6 {Question: Black's
queen stands on e6, defended once. Count the material before you capture. Tal already gave
a knight with 17.Nd4, and now takes the queen for a rook. Knight and rook for queen is
roughly level material, but 19...fxe6 20.Bd6 ties up the black rooks, the e6 pawn is weak,
and White's queen and bishop run riot. The engine gives about +4.} 19... fxe6 20. Bd6 Rfd8
21. Bc7 Rf8 22. Nb5 {Every white piece is active. Black's rooks are passive.} 22... Be5
23. Bxe5 Nxe5 24. Qxa7 Nd5 25. Qd4 Ng6 26. h4 Ngf4 27. Qe4 {Black resigned.} 1-0

[Event "Botvinnik – Tal, World Championship 1960, game 6"]
[White "Botvinnik"]
[Black "Tal"]
[Date "1960.??.??"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{Practical chances. Tal's sacrifices were often 'unsound' but very hard to answer at the
board. Tal won the title in this match. This game holds his most famous knight sacrifice.
It's an honest lesson: the engine doesn't like it, but Botvinnik couldn't solve it.} 1. c4
Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. d4 d6 6. Nc3 Nbd7 7. O-O e5 8. e4 c6 9. h3 Qb6 10.
d5 cxd5 11. cxd5 Nc5 12. Ne1 Bd7 13. Nd3 Nxd3 14. Qxd3 Rfc8 15. Rb1 Nh5 16. Be3 Qb4 17.
Qe2 Rc4 18. Rfc1 Rac8 19. Kh2 f5 {Opens the f-file toward White's king.} 20. exf5 Bxf5 21.
Ra1 Nf4 {Question: Your knight can go to f4, where a pawn can take it. Would you? Tal's
most famous practical sacrifice. The engine prefers 21...Nf6 and rates the knight
sacrifice as slightly worse for Black. But after 22.gxf4 exf4 Black's bishops come alive
and the f-pawn rolls forward. Over the board, that was too much to handle.} 22. gxf4 exf4
23. Bd2 Qxb2 24. Rab1 f3 {A pawn fork: it attacks the queen on e2 and the bishop on g2.}
25. Rxb2 fxe2 26. Rb3 Rd4 27. Be1 Be5+ 28. Kg1 Bf4 29. Nxe2 Rxc1 30. Nxd4 Rxe1+ 31. Bf1
Be4 32. Ne2 Be5 33. f4 Bf6 34. Rxb7 Bxd5 35. Rc7 Bxa2 36. Rxa7 Bc4 37. Ra8+ Kf7 38. Ra7+
Ke6 39. Ra3 d5 40. Kf2 Bh4+ 41. Kg2 Kd6 42. Ng3 Bxg3 43. Bxc4 dxc4 44. Kxg3 Kd5 45. Ra7 c3
46. Rc7 Kd4 {Black's king and c-pawn decide the endgame. White resigned.} 0-1

[Event "Tal – Larsen, Candidates match 1965, game 10"]
[White "Tal"]
[Black "Larsen"]
[Date "1965.??.??"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{Opposite-side castling against the Sicilian: speed, open files, and sacrifices to open
lines. The deciding game of a Candidates match. A Sicilian Scheveningen with opposite-side
castling.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nc3 d6 6. Be3 Nf6 7. f4 Be7 8. Qf3
O-O 9. O-O-O {Opposite-side castling. Now it's a race.} 9... Qc7 10. Ndb5 Qb8 11. g4 {The
pawn storm starts.} 11... a6 12. Nd4 Nxd4 13. Bxd4 b5 14. g5 Nd7 15. Bd3 b4 16. Nd5
{Question: Black's queen is on b8, far from his king. Can you force open the e-file? The
knight is offered to open the e-file toward e7. The engine thinks a calmer move was just
as good, but the game shows how hard this is to defend. Black's pieces are on the
queenside, far from the action.} 16... exd5 17. exd5 f5 18. Rde1 Rf7 19. h4 Bb7 20. Bxf5
{Another piece thrown in to destroy the pawn cover.} 20... Rxf5 21. Rxe7 Ne5 22. Qe4 Qf8
23. fxe5 Rf4 24. Qe3 Rf3 25. Qe2 Qxe7 26. Qxf3 dxe5 27. Re1 Rd8 28. Rxe5 Qd6 29. Qf4 Rf8
30. Qe4 b3 31. axb3 Rf1+ 32. Kd2 Qb4+ 33. c3 Qd6 34. Bc5 Qxc5 35. Re8+ Rf8 36. Qe6+ Kh8
37. Qf7 {Mating threats everywhere. Larsen resigned, and Tal won the match.} 1-0

[Event "Tal – Hecht, Varna Olympiad 1962"]
[White "Tal"]
[Black "Hecht"]
[Date "1962.??.??"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "KNIGHT'nMARE coach notes (engine-checked with Stockfish)"]

{A 'sacrifice' that is really a calculated line: count what you get back. Tal lets his
queen be taken, because he has seen that he wins it straight back.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3.
Nf3 b6 4. Nc3 Bb4 5. Bg5 Bb7 6. e3 h6 7. Bh4 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 d6 9. Nd2 e5 10. f3 Qe7 11. e4
Nbd7 12. Bd3 Nf8 13. c5 {Opens the position while Black's king is still in the centre.}
13... dxc5 14. dxe5 Qxe5 15. Qa4+ {Check, and the queen stands on a4, where Black can hit
it with ...b5.} 15... c6 16. O-O Ng6 17. Nc4 Qe6 18. e5 {Question: If Black plays ...b5
your queen on a4 is lost. Do you care? Tal pushes on: 18...b5 19.exf6 bxa4 20.fxg7 Rg8
21.Bf5 traps the black queen on e6. After 21...Nxh4 22.Bxe6, the material is back, and
White has active pieces and a strong pawn on g7. The engine rates 18.e5 as the best move.}
18... b5 19. exf6 bxa4 20. fxg7 Rg8 21. Bf5 Nxh4 22. Bxe6 Ba6 23. Nd6+ Ke7 24. Bc4 Rxg7
25. g3 Kxd6 26. Bxa6 Nf5 27. Rab1 f6 28. Rfd1+ Ke7 29. Re1+ Kd6 30. Kf2 c4 31. g4 Ne7 32.
Rb7 Rag8 33. Bxc4 Nd5 34. Bxd5 cxd5 35. Rb4 Rc8 36. Rxa4 Rxc3 37. Ra6+ Kc5 38. Rxf6 h5 39.
h3 hxg4 40. hxg4 Rh7 41. g5 Rh5 42. Rf5 Rc2+ 43. Kg3 Kc4 44. Ree5 d4 45. g6 Rh1 46. Rc5+
Kd3 47. Rxc2 Kxc2 48. Kf4 Rg1 49. Rg5 {Active rooks and the g-pawn decide. Black
resigned.} 1-0

[Event "Averbakh – Tal, USSR Championship 1958 (Benoni)"]
[White "Averbakh"]
[Black "Tal"]
[Date "1958.??.??"]
[Result "0-1"]
